From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:05:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 84/85] NextBSD: file-backed kernel dumper: crash dumps to a
 pinned UFS file

Crash dumps need a swap or dump partition today. Let dumpon(8) name a
preallocated regular file on UFS, such as /private/var/vm/kernelcore,
and write the dump through the file's physical extent map at panic
time, as xnu's kern_open_file_for_direct_io() does.

- The pinning and extent-map code of the swap-file backend is
  generalized into vn_extmap_pin(), vn_extmap_unpin() and
  vn_extmap_lookup() (swap_pager.c, declared in swap_pager.h), and the
  swap-file backend now uses them. Pinning is unchanged: VOP_FSYNC,
  vinvalbuf, a hole-free VOP_BMAP walk, then VIRF_SWAP, so the file
  cannot be written, truncated, unlinked or renamed. A file is pinned
  by swap or by the dumper, never both.
- New kern/kern_dumpfile.c. DIOCSKERNELDUMP on an open regular file
  (vn_ioctl) pins it and asks the filesystem's own g_vfs consumer for
  GEOM::kerneldump, which returns the disk's polled dump routine with
  the partition offset added. The registered dumperinfo has
  mediaoffset 0, mediasize the file's whole pages, a 4096-byte block
  (what savecore(8) uses for a regular file) and the leaf's maximum
  I/O size. No struct dumperinfo change is needed: netdump already
  registers a dumper that ignores media offsets.
- dumpfile_write() maps each write through the extent table, splits
  it at extent boundaries and calls the leaf at leaf.mediaoffset plus
  the physical offset. It takes no locks and allocates nothing. The
  final (NULL, 0, 0) flush goes to the leaf as is. EKCD encryption and
  gzip/zstd compression sit above dump_write() and need no change.
- The dumper is named by the file's absolute path, so "dumpon -l"
  prints a name savecore(8) and "dumpon -r" accept. A file can back
  several dumper configurations and stays pinned while any of them is
  registered. dumper_destroy() tells kern_dumpfile.c when a copy goes
  away ("dumpon -r", "dumpon off", a failed insert), and a task unpins
  the file after the last one.
- dounmount() removes the dumpers of files on the mount and unpins
  them before VFS_UNMOUNT, so the dumper never outlives the mount.
  Unlike a swap file, a dump file does not hold the mount.

On a partitioned disk the GEOM::kerneldump request needs the next
patch, which lets g_part answer it on a freebsd-ufs partition; without
it dumpon fails there with ENODEV and nothing else changes.

Only UFS. ZFS needs its own dump path and is left for a follow-up.
No structure layout or existing function signature changes; the new
interfaces are additions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
